// Package rules holds the markdown the MCP server and the REST API hand to
// agents: the server instructions and the runtime contract. It lives in its
// own package so the service layer (internal/app) can use it without
// importing internal/mcp, which imports the service layer.
// OWNER: MCP agent.
package rules

import (
	_ "embed"
	"strings"
)

//go:embed instructions.md
var instructions string

// Instructions returns the server instructions with the example address
// replaced by the real app address pattern (for example
// https://<slug>.bitploy.nl). exampleURL is cfg.AppURL("<slug>"), domain is
// cfg.AppsDomain.
func Instructions(exampleURL, domain string) string {
	s := strings.Replace(instructions, "https://<slug>.bitploy.nl", exampleURL, 1)
	return strings.ReplaceAll(s, "bitploy.nl", domain)
}

// RuntimeContract is what every deployed app must follow, plus the playbook
// an agent needs after the first deploy (updates, env vars, fix loops).
const RuntimeContract = `## Runtime contract

- Listen on 0.0.0.0:$PORT. bitploy sets PORT, BITPLOY_APP and BITPLOY_URL. The port is the app's port setting, otherwise the single EXPOSE of the last stage of the Dockerfile.
- Log to stdout and stderr; build and runtime logs come from get_deployment and get_logs.
- Save files the app must keep only in /data, with the volume on (create_app volume=true, or update_app volume=true then restart_app). Everything else in the container is lost on redeploy. Turning the volume off (update_app volume=false) deletes the files in /data and all backups, so it needs delete_data=true, which you pass only after the user agrees. The app user must be able to write there: in the Dockerfile run mkdir /data and chown it to the app user before USER (for example RUN mkdir /data && chown node /data). Docker copies that ownership into the new volume. Each volume has a storage limit (volume_mb, default 5 GB) taken from the account's storage allowance, which also holds every app's own files (kept images and stored source); bitploy measures /data hourly, warns at 80% and stops the app above 100%.
- Apps run as one locked-down container: 512 MB memory and 0.5 CPU by default, no root privileges, no access to the Docker socket or the host. A static site has a fixed 64 MB and 0.5 CPU, no /data, and does not count towards the memory and CPU quota. A site is static when bitploy builds it itself: send plain files, or a front end that builds to files (Vite, React, Vue, Astro, SvelteKit static, Next.js export), with NO Dockerfile. Your own Dockerfile is never static, even FROM nginx, so do not write one for a site.
- A worker (type=worker: bot, queue consumer) has no route and no port check; it counts as healthy when it is still running after 30 seconds. Its process must keep running in the foreground.
- A web app is healthy when its port is open and GET health_path (default /) answers with a status below 500 within 60 seconds. A Dockerfile HEALTHCHECK replaces this check.
- A failed deploy never takes a working app down: the old version keeps serving until the new one is healthy.
- With a volume attached, a deploy stops the old container before starting the new one (a few seconds of downtime).
- The last 3 images are kept per app so rollback works.

## Databases

- Default: SQLite with the file in /data (for example /data/app.db) and the volume on, inside the app's one container.
- The code expects Postgres or MySQL (DATABASE_URL=postgres://..., Prisma provider "postgresql")? Moving to SQLite is often a small change in the ORM (Prisma, Drizzle, Django, Laravel, SQLAlchemy); ask the user first. If they want to keep Postgres or MySQL: when the code already has a compose file with that database, deploy it as a compose app (see Compose apps); otherwise add the database with add_service (see Projects and database services). An external hosted database (Neon, Supabase...) also works: set DATABASE_URL with set_env.
- Never install a database server (postgres, mysql, mariadb, redis) in the image or run several processes in one container.
- To look at the data, call query_db(app, path, sql). It is always read-only. For SQLite, path is the file relative to /data (like app.db; find it in the code or with list_files, do not guess); start with SELECT name, sql FROM sqlite_schema. For a PostgreSQL, MySQL or MariaDB service from add_service, query_db works too: leave path out for its own database (or pass a database name) and start with the tables from information_schema.tables (PostgreSQL) or SHOW TABLES. Use it before set_public_port when the user wants to look at their data.
- To change data, call write_db with the same arguments (deploy token), only when the user asked, and create_backup first for anything risky. The user can also browse, edit and query the database in the dashboard (Storage tab).
- Run database migrations with before_deploy_command (update_app or create_app), not in CMD: for example npx prisma migrate deploy, php artisan migrate --force, python manage.py migrate --noinput. It runs on every deploy before the new version takes traffic, in a one-off container of the new image with the app's variables, network and /data. If it fails (or takes over 10 minutes) the deploy stops, the old version keeps running, and the output is in the deploy log (lines marked [before-deploy]). Rollbacks do not run it, so migrations must keep working with the previous version for a while (add columns before using them, drop them a release later). The Laravel and Django templates migrate in their start script; that is fine for SQLite in /data, move it to before_deploy_command when the database is a service. after_deploy_command runs in the live container once the new version serves (a failure is only a warning). Apps with several services (compose) have neither.
- SQLite is safe here: with a volume a deploy stops the old container before the new one starts, and create_backup freezes the app while it copies, so backups are consistent. A before_deploy_command is the one exception: it runs in a one-off container on the same /data while the old version still serves, and SQLite's file locking keeps those two writers apart (a long migration may make the live app wait for a moment).
- Need Redis for cache or queues? Laravel: CACHE_STORE=file and QUEUE_CONNECTION=sync (the laravel template sets both: there is no separate queue worker). Elsewhere an in-process cache or a SQLite-backed queue. When the code needs Redis itself, add_service template=valkey (Redis-compatible, gives REDIS_URL).

## Projects and database services

- Every app is in a project (get_app: project.id, alias). create_app without project makes one named after the app; put related apps in one project with create_app project=<id>.
- add_service(project, template, connect_to=[<slug>]) adds a PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, ClickHouse (analytics), Valkey (Redis), Meilisearch or Typesense (search), Garage (S3 storage) or Mailpit (test inbox for outgoing email) from list_service_templates as an app of its own: private (no web address), data in its own volume, passwords generated. Each connect_to app gets the main variable as a reference, like DATABASE_URL=${{postgres.DATABASE_URL}} (REDIS_URL for valkey, S3_ENDPOINT for garage, SMTP_URL for mailpit; set the others, like S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=${{garage.S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID}}, with set_env), and is restarted once the service runs. Add it before the app's first deploy: that deploy waits up to 5 minutes for the service. connect_to takes slugs; list_projects shows each app's alias and slug.
- The apps of a project reach each other at <alias>:<port> (internal_host). A value ${{<alias>.VAR}} in set_env is filled in when the app starts; the variable must be one of the service's connection_vars (like PGHOST, REDIS_PASSWORD) or a shared variable of the other app. A compose file may not contain ${{...}} (compose_reference_unsupported): set_env NAME=${{<alias>.VAR}} on the app and write ${NAME} in the file.
- A database service needs no code: never deploy_files to it. deploy with only app= rebuilds it, restart_app restarts it, create_backup backs it up (PostgreSQL, MySQL and MariaDB: a dump of every database made while it runs; Valkey, Redis, Meilisearch, Typesense and Mailpit get none; ClickHouse and Garage get a copy of their files; see Backups).
- template_update_available on a service means this bitploy has a newer version than it runs (template_latest). It is never upgraded automatically: a new major version can need a dump and restore. There is no upgrade tool for services; mention it only when the user asks. A one-click app (deploy_catalog_app) with catalog_update set is different: update_catalog_app installs the newer version when the user wants it (create_backup first).
- An app cannot move to another project (project_move_unsupported). To have it there: create_app project=<id>, set its variables, send the same code, and delete the old app only when the user asks. Files in /data and databases do not move along: the user downloads a backup in the old app's Storage tab, then uploads and restores it in the new app's.
- rename_project renames a project. delete_project needs confirm equal to the project's name; a project with apps is refused (project_not_empty) unless with_apps=true, which deletes those apps and their data too: ask the user first and name the apps.
- delete_app of an app that others refer to is refused with app_in_use; the message names those apps and their variables (details.dependents). Tell the user, and only with their go-ahead change those variables with set_env (or delete those apps), then delete again.
- deploy_catalog_app project=<id> adds a catalog app (and the databases it brings) to an existing project; a database name that is taken there gets -2, and the app's variables follow.

## Compose apps (several services)

- Only for code that already has a compose file (compose.yaml, compose.yml, docker-compose.yaml, docker-compose.yml) whose services it needs, such as a web app next to Postgres or Redis. Never write a compose file only to deploy, and never add one to code that works as one Dockerfile.
- First call validate_compose with the file's text: it lists the services, volumes, totals and the quota, and every error with code, service, key and hint. Fix every error and read every warning.
- Then create_app build_mode=compose (compose_path when the file has another name or folder), set_env for each ${VAR} the file reads (bitploy does not read .env files; pass service= for a variable of one service only), except in image: (compose_image_variable), and send the code with the compose file and every Dockerfile it builds (git, deploy_files or create_upload). A file with only image: services can be sent alone with deploy_files.
- An app made with create_app (build_mode dockerfile) never runs a compose file by itself: a deploy builds the Dockerfile and warns compose_ignored when the code's compose file has several services. Only build_mode auto picks such a file on the app's first deploy, and never switches later. Ask the user before update_app build_mode=compose. That switch keeps the app's /data (it moves to the volume mounted at /data, preferably in the public service) and cannot be undone.
- One service is public: the one with x-bitploy.public: true, else the one with ports (a built service wins over images), else the one with expose; none: every service is a worker. It gets the address and the health check (x-bitploy.health_path, else /, or its healthcheck:). The others are reached by service name on the app's own network (DATABASE_URL=postgres://db:5432/...); their ports are never published.
- Build services are built one at a time; image: services are pulled (pin a tag, like postgres:16-alpine). Services start in depends_on order (condition: service_healthy waits for the healthcheck). A service whose image and settings did not change keeps running. A failed deploy restores the old containers.
- Limits: at most 6 services. Memory per service from deploy.resources.limits.memory (default 256 MB, 512 MB for a public build service), CPU from deploy.resources.limits.cpus (default 0.25); the sum must fit the quota. Named volumes become storage (x-bitploy.size_mb, default 5 GB each); bind mounts of host folders, privileged, cap_add, devices, network_mode, pid and the Docker socket are refused (compose_forbidden). Each volume is backed up on the backup schedule; query_db takes volume= for a SQLite file outside the first volume.
- Settings of a compose app (port, memory, CPU, volumes, health path) come from the file: change it and deploy again; update_app refuses them (set_per_service).

## Limits

- deploy_files: at most 2 MB in total and 200 files. Anything bigger: zip + create_upload.
- Zip upload: at most 95 MB compressed, 500 MB unpacked, 20,000 files. If everything sits in one top-level folder, that folder is stripped. Leave out node_modules, .git, .env and build output.
- Git: shallow clone of one branch, at most 500 MB, HTTPS only. Public repositories work as is. A private GitHub repository needs the user to open the dashboard, go to Settings > GitHub, press Connect GitHub and give bitploy access to the repository; you cannot do that yourself, ask the user. If GitHub is not set up on this bitploy yet, only an admin can do that (Admin page); until then only public repositories work.
- A build takes at most 30 minutes. The built image must be under 2 GB. Images (3 kept per app) and stored source count towards the account's storage; when it is full, new builds are refused with storage_full. Keep images small: a slim base image, and no big files (videos, datasets) in the source.
- Deployments per hour, number of apps, memory, CPU, storage and custom domains per app are limited per account: whoami shows the numbers.

## Updating an app later (also from a new session)

- Which app is this code for? (1) A .bitploy file in the code folder (JSON like {"app":"quiet-otter-3f9a","url":"https://..."}) names it: update that app. (2) No .bitploy, but the user says the code is already online: find it with list_apps and confirm with the user by name and URL; if several could match, ask the user which. (3) Otherwise create a NEW app. Never reuse an app only because its name looks similar: a different codebase may have an app with the same name.
- After create_app (and after the first successful deploy), save {"app":"<slug>","url":"<url>"} as .bitploy in the code folder. Do not send that file with the deploy.
- An app linked to a git repository (set_git_source; get_app shows git_source) is updated by committing and pushing. With push_ready true every push to the branch starts a deployment by itself: follow it with get_deployment (app=<slug>, wait_seconds=30), no deploy call needed. Call deploy only to build the first time, when auto-deploy is off (push_ready false) or to rebuild the same commit. A linked app refuses deploy_files, create_upload and deploy with an upload_id (git_source_linked); to go back to uploads on purpose call set_git_source with remove=true first.
- Code with a GitHub remote is best linked with set_git_source. bitploy builds what is pushed, not local files: commit and push first. Code without a GitHub remote is uploaded: never ask the user to create a repository or install git only to deploy; at most mention once, briefly, that connecting GitHub makes updates automatic.
- deploy_files is a full snapshot, not a patch: send ALL files again; the previous source is replaced. For a zip source, create_upload again and deploy with the new upload_id. deploy with only app= rebuilds the last stored source, which is how you apply new env vars or settings to an app that is not running.
- The URL stays the same across deploys.

## Applying changes

- set_env: new values reach a running app only after restart_app (it recreates the container; no rebuild, a few seconds), or on the next deploy for a variable the build reads (see Build variables). If the app is failed, new or stopped-by-crash, call deploy (with only app=) instead, because restart_app needs a deployment that already ran. list_env shows the variables and their values; a hidden one (set_env hidden; by default a new variable whose name contains SECRET, TOKEN, KEY or PASSWORD) shows only masked.
- Build variables: every variable also reaches the image build; there is nothing to choose. bitploy's own builds (a site or Node.js app sent without a Dockerfile) give all of them to the build script, so Vite (VITE_*), Next.js (NEXT_PUBLIC_*) and Astro (PUBLIC_*) read them. A Dockerfile of your own reads one with ARG NAME on a line before the RUN step that uses it (a value the front end bakes in), or, for a key only the build needs (SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN, a private package registry token), with RUN --mount=type=secret,id=NAME,env=NAME <command>, which keeps it out of the image. A change to a variable the build reads applies on the next deploy (deploy with only app=), not on restart_app; set_env says which. Values with those front-end prefixes end up in the built files, readable by every visitor: never a secret there; tell the user such a key is public, or move its use to a server app.
- update_app volume=true, memory_mb, cpu_millis: apply on restart_app. volume=false applies at once. port, dockerfile_path, context_dir, type: apply on the next deploy. health_path: next deploy. slug (rename): applies at once, the URL changes, the app is down for a few seconds, files in /data are kept. volume_mb (storage limit): applies at once.
- Code or Dockerfile changes: a push (linked repository), or a new deploy_files or deploy. Only the Dockerfile changed in an app deployed from files: get_dockerfile reads it (also without a checkout), set_dockerfile saves the new one and deploys; every other file stays.
- cancel_deployment(id or app) stops a queued or in-progress deployment: the build ends, the new container goes and the live version keeps running. If a build is clearly wrong or stuck, call cancel_deployment instead of waiting for it, then fix the cause and deploy again. It is refused once the deployment is swapping (too_late_to_cancel) or finished (deployment_finished). A cancelled deployment is not a failure and does not count towards "3 failures".
- rollback(app, deployment_id) runs an older image again; pick a deployment from list_deployments with state running or superseded (only the last 3 images exist). Roll back only when the user asks. Only the code goes back: variables, settings, /data and database contents stay as they are, and the deploy commands do not run (a migration stays applied).

## Fixing failures (read error.code, error.hint and log_tail of get_deployment)

- dockerfile_missing: get_template, save Dockerfile and .dockerignore, deploy_files with ALL files again. Only when you cannot change the code (a GitHub repository that is not the user's, or one you have no checkout of) and it is a Node.js app or a front end: update_app build_mode=auto, then deploy with only app=; bitploy builds it without a Dockerfile.
- nothing_to_build (build_mode auto): no Dockerfile and nothing bitploy builds by itself. A Node.js app needs package.json with a start script (or main); other languages need a Dockerfile. If you cannot change the code, tell the user.
- before_deploy_failed / before_deploy_timeout: the before-deploy command (migrations) failed or did not finish in 10 minutes; the old version keeps running. Its output is in log_tail (lines marked [before-deploy]; all of it with get_logs kind=build). Usual causes: a database variable that is missing or wrong (list_env; the database service must be running), a typo in the command (exit code 127), or the migration itself. A wrong command or variable: update_app before_deploy_command=... or set_env, then deploy with only app=. A wrong migration: fix the code and send it again.
- after_deploy_failed (a warning, not a failure): the after-deploy command failed and the new version stays live. Read the [after-deploy] lines with get_logs kind=build, fix the command or the code; it runs again on the next deploy.
- port_unknown / port_ambiguous: put one EXPOSE <port> in the last stage, or call update_app with port=<number>. Without any EXPOSE bitploy uses the port the built image declares (port_unknown only when the image declares none). A bot with no web port: create the app with type=worker.
- build_failed: read the last lines of log_tail: usually a missing file the Dockerfile copies, a wrong package name, a lock file that is missing (npm ci needs package-lock.json), or a base image tag that does not exist. node-gyp, better-sqlite3, bcrypt or sharp errors: use a Debian image (FROM node:22-bookworm-slim) in every stage that installs packages, not an -alpine one; do not install compilers with apk add. "requires go >= X": the golang: image is older than a module needs; raise the tag to X or add ENV GOTOOLCHAIN=auto, and ship go.sum instead of running go mod tidy in the Dockerfile. Prisma on Alpine: "RUN apk add --no-cache openssl". Fix and deploy again.
- unhealthy and the logs mention localhost or 127.0.0.1: the app listens on the wrong address. Make it listen on 0.0.0.0 and the PORT variable.
- unhealthy and the app "stopped right after starting": the program crashed. The log_tail shows why. A missing environment variable (KeyError, "undefined", "is required") means: set_env, then deploy with only app=.
- unhealthy and "ran out of memory": raise memory_mb with update_app (check whoami for the quota), then deploy; or make the app use less.
- unhealthy and "did not answer GET /...": the app is too slow to start or returns 500 on that path. Point health_path at a cheap path that answers below 500 (for example /healthz) with update_app, then deploy. Or fix the route.
- deploy_rate_limited: wait the minutes the hint names; tell the user. Do not retry in a loop.
- token_read_only: the token cannot change anything. Ask the user for a token with the deploy scope (dashboard, Tokens).
- github_access_missing: bitploy cannot read the GitHub repository. Check the address first. If it is private, ask the user to open the dashboard, go to Settings > GitHub, press Connect GitHub and give bitploy access to this repository (the error says what to do when GitHub is not set up), or to make the repository public. Then call set_git_source again. If the user cannot do either, upload the files instead (deploy_files or create_upload): a failed set_git_source links nothing.
- git_source_linked: the app deploys from a repository. Push your change instead (get_deployment with app=<slug> follows the deployment it starts). Only when the user wants uploads again, call set_git_source with remove=true first.
- After 3 failures with the same code, stop and explain the problem to the user in plain words.

## Password protection

set_password_protection(app, enabled, username, password) puts a browser login prompt in front of the app's web addresses (its bitploy address and every custom domain). Use it only when the user asks, for example "keep this private until it is ready". Get the username and password from the user; if they have none, suggest a password of a few words and a number and say it once in your answer. Nobody can read the password back; to change it, call the tool again. A protected app answers 401 to anyone without the login. That is not a failure, and bitploy's own health checks still work. enabled=false removes the prompt.

## Custom domains

add_domain returns DNS records. Tell the user in plain words: "Open the place where you bought the domain (GoDaddy, Namecheap, Cloudflare, TransIP...), find DNS settings, add these records exactly, wait 5 to 30 minutes, then tell me." Then call check_domain until the status is active. For a root domain (example.com) the records are A records; for www.example.com a CNAME. On Cloudflare the record must be DNS only (grey cloud).

## Backups of /data

- create_backup(app) saves the /data folder of an app that has the volume on and has been deployed (an app with several volumes: one backup per volume, or volume= for one). It runs in the background (the app is frozen for a moment while its files are read): call list_backups until the backup is ready or failed (error says why). Backups count towards the account's storage allowance. Offer one before a risky change to an app that keeps data in /data; do not make them in a loop.
- A PostgreSQL, MySQL or MariaDB service (add_service) is backed up as a dump instead: pg_dumpall, mariadb-dump or mysqldump runs inside the running database and saves every database as SQL (format dump, a .sql.gz; tool says which version). The database keeps serving. A stopped database gets a copy of its files (format volume). Manual and scheduled backups both work this way. Valkey, Redis, Meilisearch and Typesense hold a cache or search index that can be rebuilt, and Mailpit holds test email: bitploy makes no backups of them (backups_not_needed; backups_supported on the app is false).
- delete_backup(app, id) deletes one backup for good and frees its space; only with the user's go-ahead.
- list_backups(app) shows the backups (format volume or dump), the automatic backup settings, whether something is running (busy) and how the latest restore went.
- Automatic backups are on by default for apps with something to back up: daily, kept 14 days, at the app's own time between 02:00 and 06:00 UTC (time_utc in the settings). set_backup_schedule(app, schedule off|every_6h|every_12h|daily|weekly|monthly, keep_mode last|days, keep) changes that; turn them off only when the user asks. Once set, bitploy never changes the schedule by itself.
- Restoring is dashboard-only: it replaces every file in /data and stops the app for a moment; a dump replaces every database of the service while it keeps running. When the user wants to go back to a backup, tell them to open the app, go to the Storage tab and press Restore there. Downloading and uploading backup files are in the same tab.
- The Storage tab also has a file browser for /data (download, upload or replace single files such as the SQLite database; make or delete folders). Send the user there to get one file out of /data or put one back. list_files(app, path) shows you the names and sizes in a folder of /data. A SQLite file there can be opened in a viewer (tables, rows, edit, SQL console).
- backup_busy: one backup or restore per app at a time; wait and try again. volume_not_ready: /data is on but the app was not started since, so restart_app first. storage_quota_exceeded or storage_full: the storage allowance is full. Call get_storage to see what takes it (space kept for /data, app files, every backup with its size), tell the user in plain words what could go, and only with their go-ahead delete_backup old backups, lower volume_mb (update_app) or delete_app. If that is not enough, tell the user to ask their bitploy admin for more storage. database_not_running: a dump is replayed by the database itself, so it must run; restart_app, wait until it runs, then the user restores again.

## Dockerfile checks (validate_dockerfile)

Errors stop the deploy: dockerfile_missing, dockerfile_parse_error, no_from, port_ambiguous (also port_unknown for an EXPOSE that cannot be read), port_reserved, base_image_forbidden, cache_mount_forbidden (RUN --mount=type=cache is not supported: the build server is shared by all apps), image_too_large.
Warnings: env_file_in_context, no_dockerignore, unpinned_base, runs_as_root, compose_ignored (the code has a compose file with several services, but only the Dockerfile is built; use SQLite in /data, or only if the user needs those services update_app build_mode=compose), database_server_in_image (use SQLite in /data, or add_service when the code needs the server), lockfile_mismatch (npm ci but the lock file is pnpm, yarn or bun), no_lockfile (package.json without a lock file: versions change on every build; ship the lock file or pin exact versions), native_module_on_alpine (better-sqlite3, bcrypt, sharp... installed on an Alpine image; pass package_json to see it). Info: no_healthcheck, port_from_image (no EXPOSE: the port comes from the image).
Fix every error and read every warning before deploying.

## Compose checks (validate_compose)

Errors stop the deploy and name the service and the compose key: compose_invalid (not valid YAML or compose), compose_no_services, compose_too_many_services (more than 6), compose_forbidden (privileged, host bind mounts, network_mode, the Docker socket...), compose_unsupported, compose_public_ambiguous (set x-bitploy.public: true on one service), compose_volume_shared (a volume mounted by two services), compose_service_name_invalid, compose_env_file_missing, compose_file_missing, compose_source_missing (a build service whose folder or Dockerfile is not in the sent files), base_image_forbidden, port_reserved, image_pull_failed, quota_exceeded, compose_reference_unsupported (a ${{<alias>.VAR}} reference in the file: set it as a variable with set_env instead, and write ${VAR} where a command needs it), compose_image_variable (image: uses a variable: write the image and tag in the file; do not set_env it), network_name_taken (<alias>-<service> is already a name in the project).
Warnings: compose_key_ignored, compose_variable_unset (a ${VAR} without a value: set_env), compose_service_skipped, compose_ports_not_published, data_volume_unmounted. Info: data_volume_kept.
`
